71:- F-74 **Under walk, an integer numeral takes its type from its value: a `ZZ32` if it fits 32 bits, a `ZZ64` if it fits 64 bits, a `ZZ` above that; it stays so until walk has the checker's static types, since without them a list of numerals and a generic's static argument take their types from the values.** Its run-time type is the library's value object `Int`, `Long` or `BigNum`, below `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` and `ZZ` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fsi:125`, `:128`, `:223`). Walk instantiates a generic at it, so a `Box[\Int\]` is a type apart from `Box[\ZZ32\]` and dispatches past it (row 364). F-74 is why P-76's walk half waits; F-79 is the library half, built. Probe Q (`compile-ladder/plan-n/probe-q/PROBE-Q.md`) measured walk with every integer numeral an `IntLiteral`, the numeral's own type in the library. It ran before climb batch N's second run and before the library half landed. Alone, the switch changed 59 test verdicts. With four devices (a numeral at an `AnyIntegral` parameter read as `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ`, a tuple of numerals converted at dispatch, `asif` converting, a varargs list converted at dispatch), the library loaded and both microGPT checks kept their values, but 24 passing interpreter tests failed: 6 by design, 3 of them team tests that assert a `ZZ32`; 9 for want of static types (rows 364, 510); and 9 walk gaps. A size used as a value is a `ZZ32` up to 2^31-1 and a `ZZ64` above (row 486; F-140). Detail: `reviews/numeral-switch-judgement.md` sections 2 and 3.
175:- F-118 **A numeral's value reaches the compiled world intact, and the compiled path prints a large `NN32` or `NN64` value unsigned, but it cannot compare a numeral beyond `ZZ64` with another numeral.** The code generator carries a numeral as an `int`, a `long` or its decimal string, by its exact bit length. So on the compiled path `4294967295` and `18446744073709551615` keep their values, as walk does at `ZZ` and `ZZ64` (walk refuses a numeral bound to `NN32` or `NN64`, F-57). The compiled path prints an `NN32` or `NN64` value above its type's signed half unsigned (F-313; test `compiler_tests/UnsignedPrintingCompiled`). It compares two `IntLiteral`s at `ZZ64`, so `a = 18446744073709551615` then `a < 5` fails with `Not in range for ZZ64` (row 328), which no test pins. An `IntLiteral` arithmetic result of 64 bits or more also keeps its value compiled: observed in the report's probe, and no test gates it. Walk stops the same program at `x + x` with `IntegerOverflow`, since its fixed-width addition raises (F-132). Gated, for the numerals at `ZZ`, `ZZ64`, `NN32` and `NN64`, by `compiler_tests/IntLiteralWrapRepairR2`. Detail: `compile-ladder/repair-r2-literal-wrap/REPORT.md`, "The failure-mode question".
194:- F-138 **Each integer type declares its own `MIN`, `MAX` and `MINMAX`, so under `walk` the family answers at the type that its arguments promote to.** It builds P-47. Each type also declares its own `MAXNUM` and `MINNUM`. Under walk an `NN32` operator with a numeral answers a `ZZ64` (row 484). Compiled, `NN32` with `ZZ32` or `ZZ64` answers `ZZ` (row 442). Gated by `tests/IntegerMinMaxRungM.fss` and `IntegerMaxNumMinNum.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-integer-minmax/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 9.
263:- F-319 **A comparison at a NaN or at 0/0 answers `false`, on the specification's word too.** It builds P-159. The overview of operators says that `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` return `false` at a floating-point NaN and at the rational 0/0, that `CMP` returns `Unordered` at a NaN, and that -1/0 and 1/0 are ordered below and above every finite rational (`Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex`, "Comparisons Operators"; Appendix I, "A comparison at a NaN or at the rational indefinite"); walk answers so, and the compiled path at a NaN (its library has no `CMP` on floats); nothing raises `FloatingComparisonError` or `RationalComparisonError`, which stay declared. Walk departs at 0/0 against itself, `true` for `=`, `<=` and `>=` and `EqualTo` for `CMP` (row 440). The team's later restart, `Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/Operators/operator-overview.tick`, still says both comparisons raise. Test: `tests/ComparisonAtNaNWalk`, and the NaN asserts of `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyFloatCompiled` and `NativeHelperFamilyRR32Compiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-nan-comparison/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 2.
